00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.260
Same thing we learned
about classifying them too.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:32.160
So if I were to put this through
a reduction reaction, usually it's

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.979
represented by an H because we're
going to be adding hydrogen to it.

00:02:36.359 --> 00:02:39.660
So usually it's either represented
with an H in brackets or maybe

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.680
with H2 being added as well.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:42.570
One or the other.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:47.390
Because the idea behind these is the only
thing changing is the functional groups.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:53.470
Now, what sets an aldose apart is that
it has an aldehyde at the very top of it.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.700
This is going to be changing.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.049
So how can that happen?

00:02:57.049 --> 00:03:00.400
Well, if you think of a double
bond as a cabinet, now, if I

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:04.050
open up the doors of the cabinet,
We're going to be adding hydrogen.

00:03:04.405 --> 00:03:06.155
to that part of the double bond.

00:03:06.805 --> 00:03:11.415
So what I like to do is when we are to
do these to help you remember oxidation

00:03:11.415 --> 00:03:18.335
reduction is I always try to color
code and change our different labels.

00:03:19.005 --> 00:03:23.824
So if I look at my double bond here,
imagine the blue line as a, as your

00:03:23.825 --> 00:03:25.615
cabinet, and we're going to open it.

00:03:26.135 --> 00:03:27.155
So what do I mean by that?

00:03:27.185 --> 00:03:31.015
Well, I'm going to open it
where one door opens on the H,

00:03:31.405 --> 00:03:33.095
the other door opened on the C.

00:03:33.525 --> 00:03:38.585
And what are you going to be adding
to both sides is a hydrogen, right?

00:03:38.595 --> 00:03:39.635
And now what did you make?

00:03:39.845 --> 00:03:43.315
Now we have a primary alcohol as a result.

00:03:43.885 --> 00:03:48.164
So that one prime alcohol
is a product of reduction.

00:03:48.735 --> 00:03:54.605
Now, specifically, this is known
as a sugar alcohol, okay, so it

00:03:54.605 --> 00:03:57.984
went from a simple sugar to a
sugar alcohol, which has a name.

00:03:58.525 --> 00:04:03.624
This is known as an alditol,
which has the ending ital Now,

00:04:03.624 --> 00:04:05.825
how can I go through and name it?

00:04:06.294 --> 00:04:07.674
Well, before it was galactose.

00:04:08.779 --> 00:04:11.540
So we're going to keep the
beginning part, and it's going to

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.939
be Galactitol as the ending, right?

00:04:14.939 --> 00:04:19.859
We have the I T O L
ending as our change here.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.879
So let's color code that so it
matches from above, and we'll do

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.990
the same thing with our os as well.

00:04:26.625 --> 00:04:27.545
All right, so that's one.

00:04:28.135 --> 00:04:32.125
Now, what about if I want to
go through the opposite way?

00:04:32.165 --> 00:04:34.735
What if I want to go through oxidation?

00:04:35.205 --> 00:04:36.844
What exactly can I do?

00:04:37.364 --> 00:04:41.674
Well, with this, we can go through
