00:04:41.734 --> 00:04:47.045
acid, which in this case is if you
go oxidation, we're adding oxygens.

00:04:47.525 --> 00:04:50.805
This is going to make
a sugar acid instead.

00:04:50.805 --> 00:04:54.495
So sugar acid, if you go one way,
sugar alcohol, if you go the other.

00:04:55.145 --> 00:04:59.575
Now, when we see this, this
oxidation process, this is known

00:04:59.575 --> 00:05:03.275
as a weak oxidation, which is
going to differ from strong.

00:05:03.925 --> 00:05:07.065
Weak oxidation, typically the
way that you see that in a lab

00:05:07.075 --> 00:05:12.244
setting is if you're adding like
Benedict's reagent, Tollens reagent.

00:05:12.715 --> 00:05:18.185
It's all about just changing a
aldehyde to a carboxylic acid.

00:05:18.495 --> 00:05:19.225
So let's try that.

00:05:19.655 --> 00:05:22.955
So if I have the following, what, the
only thing that's going to be changing

00:05:22.984 --> 00:05:27.655
is the functional group, which is on
top, and we're going to be adding an OH

00:05:27.705 --> 00:05:31.395
to it to help make that carboxylic acid.

00:05:31.835 --> 00:05:32.845
all the way at the top.

00:05:32.845 --> 00:05:36.685
So see the C double bond O
and the OH attached to it.

00:05:37.585 --> 00:05:39.635
So how would I change the name?

00:05:40.045 --> 00:05:42.075
Well, before it was galactose.

00:05:42.715 --> 00:05:46.384
So it's going to, we're going to
keep the galact part, and then

00:05:46.385 --> 00:05:52.235
we will change the ending cold to
galactonic acid as the name, right?

00:05:52.235 --> 00:05:56.484
So just like, so, so we have
our sugar acid and on the far

00:05:56.484 --> 00:05:59.085
left, we have the sugar alcohol.

00:05:59.205 --> 00:06:01.744
And in the middle, we just
